WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.790
Sometimes, small, unassuming moments in a
movie don't reveal their significance until

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:05.280
later.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.840
Here's a look at the most rewarding not-so-minor
moments in great pictures those seemingly

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.100
random, throwaway details that happened to
be a lot more important than you realized.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.100
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:21.800
J.J. Abrams' 2009 Star Trek reboot wowed audiences
with a killer explanation for presenting the

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:27.000
original Enterprise crew played by younger
actors: it takes place in an alternate timeline.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.920
One clue to the film's timeline-shifting conceit
comes in the opening scene.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.740
Characters can briefly be seen using communicators,
which look almost identical to the ones from

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.280
the original Star Trek series.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.200
When Nero jumped timelines in order to stage
his assault, his incursion had consequences.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.600
After his attack, the timeline was subtly
altered, which manifests in the communicators

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.660
looking completely different from the originals
every time we see them after that point in

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:51.220
the movie.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:56.020
John Carpenter's The Thing is an absolute
master class in suspense, and much of the

00:00:56.030 --> 00:01:00.590
film's tension is mined from the shape-shifting
nature of its alien menace and the isolated

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:02.900
setting of an Antarctic research base.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.040
Trapped together in close quarters, those
stationed at the base are forced into a deadly

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:11.360
game of hide and seek with a predator capable
of impersonating any living organism.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.160
Although, it's a situation they could easily
have avoided if any member of their team spoke

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:16.660
Norwegian.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:20.400
The film opens with a helicopter tracking
and attempting to kill a dog as it flees over

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:21.120
the tundra.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.260
It makes its way to the American base, where
the puzzled Americans understand nothing of

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:39.440
the situation other than that the Norwegians
are obviously in a blind panic.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:54.340
Unfortunately, he's shot dead before he can
try to explain what's going on, and the Americans

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.110
have no idea that he was actually saying:
Anyone who speaks Norwegian would have known

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:59.630
he was giving them a warning, saying:

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.170
"Get the hell away from that thing!

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.170
That's not a dog, it's some sort of thing!

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:04.980
It's imitating a dog, it isn't real!

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.060
Get away, you idiots!"

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.119
Given the film's bleak and ambiguous ending,
it just might have been the misunderstanding

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.260
that doomed the planet.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:16.360
In Get Out, Chris, a black man, is invited
to the home of his white girlfriend parents

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.430
only to discover that the family is involved
in a nefarious plot to hijack the bodies of

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.120
healthy young black people to host the minds
of rich, elderly whites.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.680
It's a shocking twist, unless you speak Swahili.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:31.940
The film's main title theme is a piece called
"Sikizila Kwa Wahenga," and its lyrics contain

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.170
an important message for Chris and the audience.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:35.820
Translated, they say:

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.480
"Brother, listen to the ancestors.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.000
Run!

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.000
You need to run far!

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.720
Listen to the truth.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.520
Brother, listen to the ancestors run, run!

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.830
To save yourself, listen to the ancestors."

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:48.980
Peele confirmed in a GQ interview shortly
before the film's release that this was fully

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:49.980
intentional, saying

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:52.190
"The words are issuing a warning to Chris.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:55.990
The whole idea of the movie is 'Get out!'
— it's what we're screaming at the character

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:56.640
onscreen."

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:58.500
"Get out."

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.260
"Sorry man."

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:03.520
"Get out!"

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.640
Robert Eggers' The Witch rewards viewers for
paying close attention, and it's also prone

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:13.300
to taking sudden and hard left turns into
the bizarre and surreal.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:17.400
Events befall the film's hapless family of
religious outcasts that seem like they can

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:21.489
only be explained by the supernatural — but
in the film's first act, it gives away the

00:03:21.489 --> 00:03:24.349
possibility that there may be a different
explanation.

00:03:24.349 --> 00:03:28.710
In an early scene, father William and son
Jonas are tending to the family's meager crop

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:32.080
of corn, which appears to have been affected
by some type of fungus.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.580
Indeed, it's been noted by some fans that
it looks like a very specific type: ergot,

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:41.470
which is known to cause a variety of symptoms
when ingested including mania, psychosis,

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:42.760
and hallucinations.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:46.470
It's even been suggested that ergot may have
played a role in the events leading up to

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:50.590
the actual Salem witch trials, and Eggers
himself all but confirmed the theory in a

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:51.590
Slate interview:

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:54.020
"There are clues about different interpretations.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:58.680
So, for example, the rot on the corn is ergot,
which is a hallucinogenic fungus, so if you

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.360
wanted to take that route, you could."

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:04.200
"Wouldst thou like to live deliciously?"

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:06.760
"Yes."

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:12.800
In the first act of Blue Ruin, we meet Dwight,
a homeless drifter living out of his battered

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:16.750
blue Plymouth sedan who exhibits signs of
profound PTSD.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:20.680
It's later revealed that Dwight's parents
were brutally shot to death in their car by

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:25.180
a local bully named Wade years earlier, and
Wade's impending release from prison has Dwight's

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:27.880
already fragile mental state at the breaking
point.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:31.740
Sharp-eyed viewers may have been able to deduce
that Dwight's life had been marred by violent

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.029
crime within the film's first few minutes.

00:04:34.029 --> 00:04:38.400
His blue Plymouth looks pretty much like any
other cheap, neglected beater car you might

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.540
see, except for one crucial detail: the bullet
holes adorning the drivers' side.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.930
It's not just a cheap beater, it's his parents'
old vehicle, the very one they were gunned

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:47.420
down in.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.020
Dwight has literally been living in the memory
of their murder, which makes his quest for

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.340
brutal revenge all the more understandable.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:58.099
Marvel's crossover blockbuster The Avengers
features a number of moments which subtly

00:04:58.099 --> 00:04:59.610
foreshadow later events.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:02.919
In one of its funniest scenes, Tony Stark
calls out a S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:05:02.919 --> 00:05:05.210
agent on the deck of the Helicarrier for not
doing his job

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:06.660
"That man is playing Galaga!

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.400
Thought we wouldn't notice, but we did."

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.960
As it turns out, the joke has a deeper resonance
than just showing how poorly staffed S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:17.460
is.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.599
Galaga is an unwinnable game in which the
player must defend against increasingly aggressive

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.240
swarms of alien invaders, not unlike the task
the Avengers face in the film's climax.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.700
The Marvel Cinematic Universe's version of
Spider-Man finally made his debut in 2016's

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:32.949
Captain America: Civil War.

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:36.819
When the Avengers are split down the middle
over the government initiative to regulate

00:05:36.819 --> 00:05:41.419
them, Peter Parker is recruited by Tony Stark
as a failsafe in the looming fight against

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:42.240
Captain America.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.720
It's the beginning of what will become a warm,
almost father-son relationship between Stark

00:05:46.729 --> 00:05:51.430
and Parker and with one nuanced line delivery,
Tom Holland clues the audience in to the fact

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:54.240
that Parker looks up to Iron Man in a most
profound way.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:55.100
"You're Spider-Boy?"

00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.020
"S-Spider-Man."

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:01.240
"Not in that onesie you're not."

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:03.260
"It's not a onesie."

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.800
The reason Peter's embarrassed doesn't come
down to his age; in the MCU, Stark is the

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.640
only hero to use the "-Man" suffix, implying
that Parker has modeled himself after his

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:14.140
idol.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:18.159
Ironically, it seems like he's got a lot more
in common with a different superhero.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:19.440
"You got heart, kid.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:20.980
Where you from?"

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:22.440
"Queens."

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:24.860
"Brooklyn."

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:31.700
In Andy Muschietti's 2017 theatrical adaptation
of It, actor Bill Skarsgård brought whole

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:35.039
new dimensions of brain-melting terror to
the part of Pennywise.

00:06:35.039 --> 00:06:39.389
From his very first appearance onscreen, he
gives off the distinct vive of a vicious,

00:06:39.389 --> 00:06:43.849
feral animal merely disguised as a clown and
also gives the audience a clue as to how it

00:06:43.849 --> 00:06:45.589
subtly manipulates its prey.

00:06:45.589 --> 00:06:50.129
Georgie, the younger brother of main protagonist
Bill, loses his paper boat in a storm drain

00:06:50.129 --> 00:06:53.589
but there's something down there, ready to
lure him in with the promise of returning

00:06:53.589 --> 00:06:54.080
it to him.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:58.280
When its eyes first appear in the darkness,
they're distinctly greenish-yellow.

00:06:58.289 --> 00:07:02.199
But when Pennywise steps out of the shadows
to reveal itself, the shade of its eyes has

00:07:02.199 --> 00:07:05.369
changed to blue the same color as his brother
Bill's eyes.

00:07:05.369 --> 00:07:06.820
“You look like a nice boy.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.400
I bet you have a lot of friends."

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:13.120
"Three, but my brother's my bestest."

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:14.200
"Where's he?"

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:18.560
It just may have been the comforting hue of
his brother's eyes that convinced Georgie

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.900
to make the fatal mistake of trusting Pennywise.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:26.260
For the capper on Hugh Jackman's 17-year run
as Wolverine, Logan showed audiences a grim

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:30.780
future for mutantkind: none have been born
for a quarter-century, seizures have driven

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:35.229
Charles Xavier's telepathy haywire, and it's
heavily implied that his episodes have killed

00:07:35.229 --> 00:07:36.840
off most of the mutants that were left.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:41.680
Xavier is being cared for by Logan and Caliban
while hiding out in an abandoned, rusted out

00:07:41.689 --> 00:07:45.909
mill — and astute viewers may have noticed
that its interior is a pretty dead-on visual

00:07:45.909 --> 00:07:47.680
reference to his earlier years.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:51.689
The many points of sunlight shining through
the holes into the dark interior of the mill

00:07:51.689 --> 00:07:56.430
are highly reminiscent of Cerebro, the machine
Xavier once used to track mutants.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:59.740
"These lights represent every living human
on the planet."

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.460
Cerebro would render the locations of all
mutants worldwide, which appeared as points

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.309
of light, in three-dimensional space.

00:08:10.309 --> 00:08:14.249
The hideout’s ceiling isn’t just a cool
throwback to the glory days of of the X-Men

00:08:14.249 --> 00:08:16.982
- oh no… that would be too easy there’s
something else going on here: ,

00:08:16.982 --> 00:08:20.999
where the lights once represented
living beings, they are now just holes…

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.720
because all of the mutants are gone.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:27.540
Pivoting from dark family tragedy to supernatural
thriller, Hereditary keeps you completely

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:30.539
unsure as to whether any of it is actually
happening.

00:08:30.539 --> 00:08:35.200
You can't really be sure if the supernatural
goings-on are really taking place or if Annie's

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.580
mental illness is manifesting in seemingly
supernatural ways.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:42.120
That is, unless we were paying attention during
her scene in the support group, when Annie

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:45.880
talks about how her brother complained about
their mother trying to put people inside of

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:46.380
him.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:50.820
It could be interpreted as schizophrenia … except
that we later find out that this is exactly

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:52.510
what Annie's mom was trying to do.

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:56.950
She was part of a coven searching for a male
host for the demon Paimon, and her attempts

00:08:56.950 --> 00:09:01.420
to put Paimon inside her son was rationalized
away as symptoms of his schizophrenia.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:07.440
2016's Arrival gave us a heady sci-fi scenario:
12 alien spacecraft descend upon different

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:08.450
points of the Earth.

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:13.120
The U.S. military recruits an expert linguist
to decipher their communications, which appear

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:14.760
as circular, complex glyphs.

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:19.320
Other nations are also in on the effort, which
takes a harrowing turn when the alien beings,

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:23.010
which have been dubbed "heptapods," appear
to make a reference to using a weapon.

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:27.000
None of the involved countries are able to
agree on the interpretation but Arrival's

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:31.150
use of communication issues are hinted early
on in the nicknames that the American team

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:32.320
gives to two aliens.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:36.460
They're called "Abbott" and "Costello," which
is obviously a reference to the famous comedy

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:41.360
duo, whose most iconic sketch, "Who's on First?"
was essentially one long series of humorous

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:42.420
miscommunications.

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:43.120
"Who's on first?"

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:43.720
"Yes."

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:44.340
"I mean, the guy's name."

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:44.920
"Who."

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:45.440
"The guy playing first."

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:45.940
"Who."

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:46.800
"The guy playing first base."

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:47.300
Who?

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:48.200
The guy playing first!

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:49.660
"Who is on first!"

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:50.600
"What're you asking me for?

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:51.600
I dunno!"

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:58.520
"What we've got here is failure to communicate."

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:03.040
The nicknames given to the creatures serves
as a clue to sharp audiences about what's

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:03.540
really going on

